Software development environments have evolved significantly over the past decades, with Integrated Development Environments (IDEs) becoming a standard programmer tool.
Modern IDEs provide powerful features such as graphical interfaces, intelligent code completion, and integrated debugging, which support developers in writing and maintaining complex software systems.

Despite these advancements, the command-line terminal remains essential to the development workflow.
It provides powerful and flexible capabilities for tasks ranging from build automation to deployment, version control, and system configuration.
The terminal complements the IDE, enabling either unavailable or less efficient operations in graphical tools.

At the Technical University of Munich, students of the subject Computer Science, Information Technology, and Games Engineering encounter this dual environment early in their studies.
The Java programming language teaches students the fundamentals of object-oriented programming, and students are expected to use both IDEs and terminals as part of their exercises.

Among modern IDEs, Theia stands out as a modern ai-native IDE for cloud and desktop built on the Theia Platform.
The Theia Platform is an open soure framework for building custom, tailored cloud & desktop IDEs that is managed by Eclipse Source. @TheiaIDEAINative

